'''''Echeveria lilacina''''', common name '''ghost echeveria''' or '''Mexican hens and chicks''', is a [[species]] of [[succulent plant]]s in the genus ''[[Echeveria]]'' belonging to the family [[Crassulaceae]].

==Description==
[[File:Echeveria lilacina 2.jpg|thumb|180px|left|Flowers of ''Echeveria lilacina'']]
''Echeveria lilacina'' can reach a height of about 15&nbsp;cm. The leaves are silvery-grey, spoon shaped, fleshy and arranged in a symmetrical rosette of 12–25&nbsp;cm of diameter. This species is slow growing and drought-tolerant. Flowers are pale pink or coral-colored. They emerge on small short arching racemes on the top of reddish stems of about 15&nbsp;cm. Flowering period extends from later winter to early spring.{{Citation needed|reason=No references in this section|date=May 2020}}

==Distribution==
This species is native to [[Nuevo Leon]], in northern [[Mexico]].{{Citation needed|reason=No references in this section|date=May 2020}}

==Habitat==
''Echeveria lilacina'' grows on rocky areas at quite high elevations.{{Citation needed|reason=No references in this section|date=May 2020}}

==Cultivation==
''Echeveria lilacina'' is cultivated as an [[ornamental plant]], for use in gardens, and as a potted plant.<ref>[http://www.smgrowers.com/products/plants/plantdisplay.asp?plant_id=2937 San Marco Growers treatment: ''Echeveria lilacina'']</ref>

==Etymology==
The genus name ''Echeveria'' was given in honor of the 18th century Mexican botanist and painter [[Atanasio Echeverría y Godoy]], famous for his paintings of plants, many of which were included in ''Flora Mexicana''.<ref name="gledhill">Gledhill, David (2008). "The Names of Plants". Cambridge University Press. {{ISBN|9780521866453}} (hardback), {{ISBN|9780521685535}} (paperback).  pp. 149, 237</ref>

''lilacina'' means 'lilac-colored'.<ref name="gledhill" />

